Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Cyclic Tag Compiler", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->373329666,ExpressionUUID->"349adac8-3631-9a4f-ad99-2aff5b2831d8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "CyclicTagCompiler compiles finite cellular automata, automata networks, \
oblivious ring machines and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "16a2c566-7355-a54e-9d37-b483877f1ef9"],
 "-ary cyclic tag systems to ordinary cyclic tag systems, and decodes the \
evolution of a cyclic tag system back to the evolution of the system it \
emulates. Cyclic tag systems are among the simplest universal systems, and \
are used in the proofs that the rule 110 cellular automaton and Wolfram's 2,3 \
Turing machine are universal."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->688461203,ExpressionUUID->"62449a4d-09d8-c64f-a4f8-824c63978991"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Cellular automata and automata networks are compiled to oblivious ring \
machines, those to ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "06626aa0-145c-ff4f-aee5-4060532cca69"],
 "-ary cyclic tag systems, and those to cyclic tag systems. No stage encodes \
numbers in unary, so the cyclic tag systems are not exponentially slower than \
the systems they emulate. Each stage can also be used on its own."
}], "GuideText",
 CellID->498029877,ExpressionUUID->"47df3454-a71f-cb41-baf6-19c418e5741d"],

Cell["\t", "GuideDelimiterSubsection",ExpressionUUID->"8e16c11b-4cf2-084f-b730-10b9b32074bf"],

